(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["interpretation-of-medical-literature-exam-1-evidence-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Evidence Quiz 2 — Interpretation of Medical Literature","category":"Interpretation of Medical Literature","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which database is maintained by the United States National Library of Medicine?","choices":["The Cochrane Library","Up-To-Date","Medline and PubMed","Clinical Key"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it sits alongside the Cochrane Library, Evidence Based Medicine Reviews and Up-To-Date among the named evidence-based resources. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What does LOW certainty mean about future evidence?","choices":["Future studies are unlikely to change the conclusion","The direction of the effect could change but not its magnitude","More information may allow effects on health outcomes to be estimated","No further research on the question is warranted"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the reasons the current evidence falls short are listed: too few or too small studies, important design flaws, inconsistency, gaps in the chain of evidence and missing outcome data. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which ethical breaches accompanied that retracted study?","choices":["Fabrication of the entire patient sample","Publication in a journal without peer review","Failure to obtain funding before starting the work","Improperly obtained blood and unapproved invasive tests"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: blood was taken from children at a birthday party for payment, and autistic children underwent colonoscopy and lumbar puncture without research review board approval. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What characterizes HIGH certainty regarding net benefit?","choices":["Evidence sufficient to determine effects, but constrained confidence","Consistent results from well-designed studies in representative populations","Evidence insufficient to assess effects on health outcomes","Evidence drawn entirely from expert consensus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the consequence is stated directly: the conclusion is unlikely to be strongly affected by the results of future studies. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What does a Grade A recommendation mean?","choices":["High certainty that the net benefit is substantial","High certainty that the net benefit is moderate","At least moderate certainty that the net benefit is small","Moderate or high certainty that there is no net benefit"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the instruction attached is to offer or provide the service. Grade B carries the same instruction on a slightly weaker evidence footing. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"In the pyramid, which design sits immediately below randomized controlled trials?","choices":["Systematic reviews","Cohort studies","Case-control studies","Expert opinion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and case-control studies with case series and reports sit below those in turn, so the order rises from case reports through case-control and cohort to randomized trials. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"On what basis does Grade D recommend against a service?","choices":["Insufficient evidence to determine the balance","Moderate or high certainty of no net benefit","At least moderate certainty that the net benefit is small","High certainty that the net benefit is moderate"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the instruction is to discourage its use. This is a positive finding against the service rather than an absence of evidence about it. Harms outweighing benefits earns the same grade. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"How is evidence-based medicine defined?","choices":["The use of whichever treatment has the largest published trial","The replacement of clinical judgment with published data","The conscientious, explicit and judicious use of current best evidence","The systematic collection of new data on every patient"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the definition continues: the practice of it means integrating individual clinical expertise with the best available external clinical evidence from systematic research. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which levels of the pyramid count as filtered information?","choices":["Systematic reviews and critically appraised topics","Randomized controlled trials and cohort studies","Case series, case reports and expert opinion","Every level above expert opinion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and filtered means someone has already appraised the primary studies for you. Randomized trials, cohort and case-control studies are unfiltered, appraised only by the reader. Critically appraised individual articles are filtered too. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which two categories sit below case reports in the version of the pyramid that includes laboratory work?","choices":["Case-control studies and cohort studies","Animal research and in vitro research","Systematic reviews and meta-analyses","Randomized controlled double-blind studies"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with in vitro research at the very bottom, and ideas, editorials and opinions sitting between animal research and case reports. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which challenge concerns how research findings are communicated?","choices":["Findings are published too frequently to keep up with","Findings are only published in languages other than English","Findings may be presented in language clinicians struggle with","Findings are withheld by journals until they are outdated"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it is a distinct problem from the evidence being absent or conflicting: the evidence exists and is sound, but does not reach the person who needs it. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What does it mean for information to be unfiltered?","choices":["Nobody has appraised the primary studies for the reader","The studies have not yet been peer reviewed","The studies have been excluded from systematic reviews","The findings have not been published"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, so the appraisal falls to whoever is reading. Randomized trials, cohort and case-control studies all sit in that tier despite being sound designs. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What is a systematic review?","choices":["A narrative summary of a field written by an expert","A single large study with a greater number of participants","A study that follows one group forward over time","Identifying and evaluating multiple studies systematically"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the two qualifiers carry the weight: the identification is systematic rather than convenient, and the question is specific rather than broad. It does so to answer one specific, focused question. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What does the Morbidity and Mortality Weekly Report carry?","choices":["Original randomized controlled trial reports","Systematic reviews of therapeutic questions","Individual state disease registry data","Public health policies for prevention and treatment"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it sits among the guideline sources alongside the Agency for Healthcare Research and Quality and the Task Force on Community Preventive Services. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What is the ACP Journal Club?","choices":["A database of raw clinical trial data","An American College of Physicians evidence resource","A federal guideline clearinghouse","A tutorial for constructing Medline searches"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and numerous other specialty journal clubs and evidence-based groups exist alongside it, together with resources such as the Clinical Advisor evidence section. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What question does assessment evidence answer?","choices":["What the characteristics of patients with a diagnosis are","Which tests best identify or predict a diagnosis","Which treatments work best in a given population","Whether a screening program reduces mortality"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it sits between descriptive evidence, which characterizes patients with a diagnosis, and treatment evidence, which asks what works against it. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What warning accompanies the advice to prefer peer-reviewed and government sources?","choices":["Be cautious of systematic reviews","Be cautious of government databases","Be cautious of websites","Be cautious of randomized controlled trials"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the appraisal methods offered exist for exactly that reason: the CRAAP test and the ABCD method give a way to judge a site that carries no prior scrutiny. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What sits at the very base of the clinical studies pyramid?","choices":["Case reports and case series","Cohort studies","Systematic reviews","Expert opinion and background information"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. One version of the pyramid puts in vitro and animal research below ideas, editorials and opinions, but the principle is the same: unappraised opinion is the weakest footing. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which of these appears among the named sources of medical information?","choices":["State disease registries","The United Nations Demographic Yearbook","Vital statistics registration","Pharmaceutical representatives"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and their presence on the list is not an endorsement: the list is deliberately broad, and quality has to be judged separately by peer review and appraisal. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What does Grade C recommend?","choices":["Offering it selectively, by judgment and preference","Offering the service to every eligible patient","Discouraging use of the service","Reading the clinical considerations before deciding"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it rests on at least moderate certainty that the net benefit is small, which is why it is offered for selected patients depending on individual circumstances. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What alternative to a full systematic review is offered at the second step?","choices":["A synopsis from a credible resource","The opinion of a senior colleague","A single randomized controlled trial","A textbook chapter on the subject"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it is the practical concession in the whole framework: searching for the best evidence is named as time consuming, so a pre-appraised synopsis is an accepted substitute. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What distinguishes a Grade I statement from a Grade D?","choices":["Grade I means the service is harmful and should be avoided","Grade I means the balance cannot be determined","Grade I applies only to services that have never been studied","Grade I carries the same instruction as Grade D"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the distinction matters in practice: evidence is lacking, poor or conflicting, so if the service is offered the patient should understand that uncertainty. It does not mean the balance is unfavorable. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What marks a quality source of medical information?","choices":["A large number of citations to the article","Publication within the last twelve months","Peer review, with government databases a further category","Endorsement by a pharmaceutical company"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the matching warning is blunt: be cautious of websites, which carry no equivalent guarantee of scrutiny before publication. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What are the three primary elements of joint clinical decision making?","choices":["Diagnosis, investigation and treatment","Searching, appraising and applying the evidence","Disclosure of risks and benefits, values, and the decision","Disclosure, documentation and follow-up"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the ordering is meaningful: the alternatives and their consequences are laid out, the patient's values about those outcomes are explored, and only then is the decision made. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What does the practice of evidence-based medicine integrate?","choices":["Clinical expertise with external evidence from systematic research","External evidence with institutional cost constraints","Clinical expertise with the opinion of senior colleagues","Published guidelines with local hospital policy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and clinical expertise is defined concretely as the proficiency and judgment gleaned from clinical practice, so it is experience disciplined by evidence rather than either alone. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What is the first step of evidence-based practice?","choices":["Systematically reviewing the literature","Evaluating the validity of the research","Evaluating performance or outcome","Creating a focused clinical question"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and everything after it depends on that focus: the review, the validity assessment, the application to the patient and the evaluation of outcome. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What is true of studies at the higher levels of an evidence hierarchy?","choices":["They involve fewer participants and so are more precise","They are published more quickly than lower-level studies","They are always experimental rather than observational","They are least vulnerable to bias and more generalizable"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and there is a third property: outcomes are more likely to be attributable to the construct actually being studied rather than to something else. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which resource incorporates InfoPOEMS and InfoRetriever?","choices":["The Cochrane Library","Essential Evidence Plus","Medline","Up-To-Date"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it sits among the evidence-based databases with TripPro and Clinical Key rather than among the general literature indexes. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which two situations both earn a Grade B?","choices":["High certainty of moderate benefit, or moderate certainty of more","High certainty of substantial benefit only","Moderate certainty of a small benefit only","Insufficient evidence to weigh benefits against harms"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and either way the instruction is the same as for Grade A: offer or provide the service. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Where are tutorials for constructing Medline searches found?","choices":["The National Library of Medicine and the university library","The Cochrane Library","The United States Preventive Services Task Force","The Agency for Healthcare Research and Quality"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and their existence reflects a real difficulty: searching and finding the best evidence is named as one of the challenges of evidence-based practice. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 24"}]};
